          "number": 9,
          "letter": "I",
          "english": "Mroz says a good place to start is asking ourselves if our meetings are even necessary in the first place. If all that's on the agenda is a quick catch-up, or some non-urgent information sharing, it may better suit the group to send around an email instead. \"The second thing I would always recommend is keep the meeting as small as possible,\" says Mroz. \"If they don't actually have some kind of immediate input, then they can follow up later. They don't need to be sitting in this hour-long meeting.\" Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to more employee engagement in the meetings they do attend, which experts agree is a proven remedy for MRS.",
          "chinese": "莫罗兹说，一个好的起点是问问自己，我们的会议是否真的有必要。如果议程上的全部内容只是快速沟通近况，或分享一些不紧急的信息，那么向大家发送一封电子邮件也许更适合。“我始终建议的第二件事，是让会议规模尽可能小，”莫罗兹说。“如果他们实际上没有什么需要立即提供的意见，那么之后再跟进就可以了。他们没有必要坐在这场长达一小时的会议里。”减少参加会议的时间，最终会让员工更加投入他们确实参加的会议，而专家一致认为，提高参与度是解决会议恢复综合征的有效方法。",
